"""Grade 12 life essentials: launching into adult life."""
from spec import L, U

N12 = U('Launching', 'Morality', 'How do I keep my faith when I leave home, when it gets hard, and when I choose to love for good?', [
L('Leaving home and keeping the faith: the first semester test',
  'Surveys show that many young Catholics drift in the first months away from home. They rarely decide to leave; they simply stop, because no one is checking and everything is new. This lesson uses the Catechism on the Sunday obligation, the precepts of the Church, community and the sacraments to build a concrete plan for the first thirty days of college, work or service, before the habit of drifting sets in.',
  '2041-2043,2177-2188,1389,1457,947-948,2226,1324',
  'Mark 8:34-38;Acts 2:42-47;Hebrews 10:23-25;Luke 24:13-35',
  'mass sunday eucharist parish church',
  'What is one habit of faith you do now mostly because your family expects it? What would remain without them?',
  'Fill in a “first thirty days plan”: where and when you will go to Mass, which Mass is closest to where you will live, the name of the campus or parish minister, one group to join, and one friend who will hold you to it. Put the Mass time in your phone calendar tonight. If you already know where you are going, look up the parish website and email a person.',
  'spiritual-communion',
  id='12-L1',
  hook='Read Luke 24:13–35, the road to Emmaus. Ask: “Where were the two disciples heading? Who walked with them? Where did they recognise him? What did they do next?” (Away from Jerusalem; Jesus; at the breaking of the bread; they went back at once.)',
  teach=[
    'The Church names the basic habits of Catholic life as the “precepts of the Church”: Sunday and holy-day Mass, Confession at least once a year, receiving Communion at least in the Easter season, fasting and abstinence on the appointed days, and providing for the Church’s needs (CCC 2041–2043). They are called the “indispensable minimum” in prayer and moral effort. They are not the goal of a faith; they are the floor under it.',
    'Sunday Mass is the heart of the matter. “The Sunday Eucharist is the foundation and confirmation of all Christian practice” (CCC 2181). “Those who deliberately fail in this obligation commit a grave sin” (CCC 2181). But the obligation protects something more than a rule: the Eucharist is the “source and summit of the Christian life” (CCC 1324), the place where Christ gives himself. Going is not a performance for your family; it is a meeting with the one who loves you.',
    'Faith is carried by people. “I believe in the communion of saints” means we belong to a family, and a family has to be found again in a new place (CCC 946–948). In Acts 2 the early Christians “devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers.” That is a pattern: teaching, friends, Mass and prayer. The writer of Hebrews tells them not to neglect meeting together, “as is the habit of some” (Hebrews 10:25).',
    'Plan for the weak points. First week: find the closest Mass and the campus ministry before the schedule fills. Month one: one fixed habit (Sunday Mass), one group, one friend. Confession before Advent or Lent. When you fail, return as soon as you can; the sacrament of Penance is for exactly that (CCC 1457). Drift is not a decision, it is a lack of one. So decide now.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What parts of your faith rely on your family? What parts would you keep alone?',
    'What makes it easy to drift in the first month away?',
    'What kind of friends and communities will help, and how will you find them?',
    'Why do you think the Church calls the Sunday Eucharist the foundation of Christian practice?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Church call the Sunday Eucharist?|Optional|The foundation and confirmation of all Christian practice|A family tradition|A suggestion|1|CCC 2181.',
    'What are the precepts of the Church?|The indispensable minimum of prayer and moral effort, such as Sunday Mass and annual Confession|Suggestions|Only for priests|Old rules no longer used|0|CCC 2041–2043.',
    'Why does faith need a community?|It does not|We belong to the communion of saints, a family that carries and supports us|Only for the weak|For social reasons only|1|CCC 946–948; Acts 2:42.',
  ],
  life='Tonight, put your first three Sundays’ Masses in your phone calendar with a reminder. Send one email or message to a campus minister or parish office. Write the name of one friend who will go with you or check on you, and tell them the plan.',
  saint='Francis Xavier: a bright young lecturer at the University of Paris whom his roommate Ignatius challenged with “What does it profit a man to gain the whole world and lose his soul?”',
  misc=[
    '“I can pray on my own, so Mass is not necessary.”',
    '“If I miss Mass once, I might as well stop.”',
    '“Going to Mass is just for my family.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:mass-sacrifice', 'apol:confession'],
  mem='“Let us consider how to stir up one another to love and good works, not neglecting to meet together.” (Hebrews 10:24–25)',
  assess='Ask each student to state the first-thirty-days plan, including where and when they will go to Mass and which person will hold them to it.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read Luke 24:13–35 aloud, slowly. Then ask: “Which way were the two disciples walking? When did they see him? And what did they do right away?” Say, “They were leaving. He walked with them. They knew him at the breaking of bread, and went back.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Most young Catholics who drift do not decide to. They get busy, no one is watching, and it slides. This lesson is about not drifting. The Church names a floor under Catholic life: the precepts of the Church. Sunday Mass, Confession at least once a year, Communion in the Easter season, fasting and abstinence, and supporting the Church. They are the minimum. Not the ceiling.”',
      'Say: “Sunday Mass is the heart. The Catechism, 2181, calls the Sunday Eucharist the foundation and confirmation of all Christian practice. Not because it is a rule, but because in the Eucharist Christ gives himself. The Mass is a meeting. If you are going only because your parents expect it, now is the moment to find out what is true for you.”',
      'Say: “Faith needs people. The Creed says ‘I believe in the communion of saints.’ In Acts 2, the first Christians held together around teaching, fellowship, bread and prayer. Hebrews says do not neglect meeting together, as some do. You will need a new group in a new place. You find it by looking, early.”',
      'Say: “So plan the weak points. Before you leave: find the nearest Mass and the campus minister. Week one: go. Month one: Mass, one group, one friend. Before Advent or Lent: Confession. And when you fall, go back right away. Drift is not a decision. It is the lack of one. Decide now.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Which part of your faith is yours, not just your family’s?',
      'What will be the hardest part of the first month?',
      'Who will hold you to your plan?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out a first-thirty-days plan: where and when you will go to Mass, who you will contact, one group to join, one friend.',
      'Put the Mass times in your phone while you are here.',
      'Write the first email or message and send it before you leave.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer of spiritual communion together. Say, “You will be far from home. He is not.”'),
  tryit=['Find the nearest Mass and put it in your calendar.', 'Write to a campus or parish contact.', 'Name the friend who will check on you.'],
  saintStory='Francis Xavier was born in 1506 in the Basque country of Spain, to a noble family. At eighteen he went to the University of Paris, where he did well and planned a career in the Church or in teaching. His roommate was Ignatius of Loyola, who was fifteen years older, a former soldier who had come to study late.\nIgnatius worked on Francis for years. Francis thought Ignatius was a bore; Ignatius kept asking him the question of Jesus: “What does it profit a man to gain the whole world and lose his own soul?” In 1533 Francis gave in. With Ignatius and five others, he took vows at Montmartre in 1534. They were the beginning of the Jesuits.\nIn 1541 Francis sailed to India as a missionary. He worked in Goa, southern India, Southeast Asia and Japan, baptizing thousands and writing letters that inspired others. He died on December 3, 1552, on the island of Sancian off the coast of China, waiting for a boat that would take him in. He was canonized in 1622 and is the patron of missions.',
),

L('When faith gets hard: doubt, God’s silence and dark seasons',
  'Almost everyone who prays seriously meets a time when nothing seems to happen, or when suffering or an argument makes belief feel foolish. The Church’s teaching on faith, doubt, the testing of faith and the long tradition of the “dark night” treat this as normal, even as a stage, and offer a way through. This lesson is for students who already feel it and for those who will.',
  '157-159,162,164,2725-2728,2734-2737,1501',
  'Psalm 22:1-5;Psalm 88;Mark 9:23-24;John 20:24-29',
  'faith hope prayer faith-reason',
  'What is one question about God you are afraid to ask?',
  'Write a “lament” in the style of the Psalms in four parts: Address (“God,”), Complaint (what is wrong), Request (what you ask), and Trust (one thing you still hold on to). Keep it honest. Use Psalm 22 as a model. Share only if you want to.',
  'jesus-prayer',
  id='12-L2',
  hook='Read Mark 9:24 aloud: “I believe; help my unbelief!” Ask: “Does Jesus scold the father? What does he do instead?” (He heals the boy.)',
  teach=[
    'Doubt is not the opposite of faith. The Catechism distinguishes between voluntary doubt, which neglects or refuses to hold as true what God has revealed, and the honest struggle of an immature or tested faith (CCC 2088). It also states that faith “is certain”, more certain than human knowledge, because it rests on God’s word, but it may feel obscure (CCC 157). “Faith seeks understanding” (CCC 158). So questions are not an enemy; they are part of how faith grows (CCC 156–159).',
    'The Church is honest about trials. “Faith is a gift... Now, however, ‘we walk by faith, not by sight’... The world we live in often seems very far from the one promised us by faith. Our experiences of evil and suffering, injustice and death, seem to contradict the Good News; they can shake our faith and become a temptation against it” (CCC 164). The Bible is full of it: Job, Jeremiah, Psalm 88, the cry of Jesus on the cross, “My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?” (Psalm 22:1). God gave us those words to pray.',
    'Prayer can go dry. The Catechism says prayer is a gift and “a battle,” and names the discouragement we feel when we “do not receive what we ask” or are not “heard” (CCC 2725–2728, 2734–2737). The answer it gives is trust: “Filial trust is tested when we feel that we are not always heard” (CCC 2734). The Church’s tradition, from Teresa of Avila to John of the Cross, speaks of dryness and the “dark night” as a stage in which God purifies faith from reliance on feeling.',
    'In practice: keep the small habits (a short prayer, Mass, a friend) when feeling is gone. Say the true thing: “I do not feel anything.” Read a Psalm of lament. Talk to someone wise: a priest, a mentor, a friend who has been there. Keep asking the question, and look for good answers in good books. And if the doubt is bound up with depression, grief or trauma, seek care for that too (CCC 2288). Thomas had his doubt answered when he stayed with the community (John 20:24–28).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Is it okay to be angry at God? What do the Psalms suggest?',
    'What is the difference between doubt that asks and doubt that refuses?',
    'What small practice would you keep when you feel nothing?',
    'Who is a wise person you could take a hard question to?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say about faith and understanding?|Faith has no use for understanding|Faith seeks understanding|Understanding replaces faith|Questions are sinful|1|CCC 158.',
    'Is feeling nothing in prayer a sign God is absent?|Yes|Not necessarily; it can be a time of testing and growth, to be met with trust|It means you are a sinner|It means you should stop|1|CCC 2734–2737.',
    'What does the Church offer in the Psalms?|Only praise|Words for lament, doubt and trust that we can pray when we cannot find our own|Rules|Nothing|1|Psalms 22 and 88.',
  ],
  life='For the next week, pray one lament Psalm (22, 13, 88 or 130) slowly each day. Then speak to one person you trust about a real question. Keep one small habit of faith, a short prayer or Sunday Mass, even if it feels empty.',
  saint='John of the Cross: a Carmelite friar who was imprisoned in a tiny cell for nine months by his own brothers, and wrote poems about the “dark night of the soul” afterward.',
  misc=[
    '“Faith means never doubting.”',
    '“If I do not feel God, I am doing something wrong.”',
    '“The Bible is not honest about doubt.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:problem-of-evil', 'apol:suffering', 'room:teresa-castle'],
  mem='“I believe; help my unbelief!” (Mark 9:24)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain the difference between doubt that seeks and doubt that refuses, and to write their four-part lament.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read Mark 9:24 aloud: “I believe; help my unbelief!” Ask, “Does Jesus scold him? What does he do?” Then say, “Today is about honest faith, the kind that has questions.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Doubt is not the opposite of faith. The Catechism separates two things: refusing to hold what God has revealed, and honestly struggling to understand. The first is a sin against faith. The second is part of growth. It also says faith is certain, more certain than human knowledge, because it rests on God. But it can feel obscure. ‘Faith seeks understanding.’ Questions are how you get there.”',
      'Say: “The Church is honest about hard times. Number 164: our experiences of evil and suffering can shake our faith. The Bible does not hide it. Job. Jeremiah. Psalm 88, which ends in darkness. Jesus on the cross quoting Psalm 22: ‘My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?’ God put these words in the Bible so we could pray them.”',
      'Say: “Prayer goes dry. The Catechism names the temptation to say, ‘The Lord does not hear.’ It says that trust is tested when we feel unheard. The saints, Teresa and John of the Cross, call this a stage. They say God is purifying faith from feeling. They also say do not stop. Keep the habit even when it is empty.”',
      'Say: “What to do. Keep small habits: a short prayer, Mass. Say the true thing: I feel nothing. Pray a lament Psalm. Talk to a wise person. Read good answers to your questions. And if the doubt is mixed with depression or grief, seek help for that too. Thomas got his answer when he stayed with the community.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What question about God are you afraid of?',
      'What small habit could you keep when you feel nothing?',
      'Who could you take a real question to?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Read Psalm 22:1–5 aloud. Note the four parts: address, complaint, request, trust.',
      'Write your own four-line lament, honestly.',
      'Do not share unless you wish to. Fold it and keep it in your Bible or your phone.',
    ],
    close='Pray the Jesus Prayer together: “Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me.” Say, “He can handle your questions.”'),
  tryit=['Write a four-part lament: address, complaint, request, trust.', 'Choose one small habit to keep.', 'Name a wise person to talk to.'],
  saintStory='Juan de Yepes, who became known as John of the Cross, was born in 1542 in Fontiveros, Spain, to a family that fell into poverty when his father died. He joined the Carmelites, and met Teresa of Avila, who was reforming the order. He joined her reform, and with her founded the first houses of the Discalced Carmelite friars.\nThe reform made enemies. On the night of December 2, 1577, some of his own brothers broke into his house in Ávila and took him to Toledo. For nine months he was kept in a cell about six feet by ten, with a tiny window high up, fed on bread and water, and beaten publicly each week. He had no light but what came through the window and no books. There he began to compose his poems in his head. In August 1578 he escaped by climbing out of a window with a rope made from blankets.\nHe wrote the Spiritual Canticle and The Dark Night, which describe how God sometimes seems to withdraw to purify our love. He died in 1591, and was canonized in 1726. In 1926 Pius XI named him a Doctor of the Church.',
),

L('Love that lasts: commitment, vows and the culture of options',
  'Young adults are the first generation with endless options: you can always swipe, move, or start again. Yet people want something that lasts. The Church’s teaching on marriage, covenant, consent, indissolubility and the domestic church offers an unusual view: that the wisest love is chosen, public, and unconditional, and that a vow is a gift of freedom and not a loss of it. The lesson is for all students, married or not, because every vocation involves vows or promises.',
  '1601-1617,1638-1651,1643-1645,1652-1658,2360-2365,2366',
  'Genesis 2:18-24;Ruth 1:16-17;Ephesians 5:21-33;Matthew 19:3-9',
  'matrimony marriage-bond covenant vocation',
  'Why do you think many people are afraid of making a lifelong promise?',
  'Write the vow you would want a person to say to you if they promised to love you, and what you would promise in return. Then underline the words that cannot be fulfilled by feeling alone (such as “always,” “in sickness,” “for better, for worse”). Discuss what practices make such promises possible.',
  'st-joseph',
  id='12-L3',
  hook='Ask: “If you could swipe, restart, or get out of anything without consequence, what would you lose?” Take a few answers. Then read Ruth 1:16–17, “Where you go I will go.”',
  teach=[
    'Marriage is a covenant, not a contract: “The matrimonial covenant, by which a man and a woman establish between themselves a partnership of the whole of life, is by its nature ordered toward the good of the spouses and the procreation and education of offspring” (CCC 1601). It is based on consent, freely given and not coerced: “I take you” (CCC 1625–1632, 1626). Consent is the cause of the marriage; love grows the marriage afterward.',
    'Why lifelong? The Catechism says: “The love of the spouses requires, of its very nature, the unity and indissolubility of the spouses’ community of persons” (CCC 1644). Love gives itself totally, and a total gift has no expiry. Conjugal love is a totality in which every part of the person enters (CCC 1643). Jesus tells the Pharisees: “What God has joined together, let not man put asunder” (Matthew 19:6; CCC 1614–1615). The promise is what makes real trust possible.',
    'This is not naive. The Church knows that vows are hard; that is why marriage is also a sacrament, giving spouses grace to live the covenant (CCC 1641–1642). It also lists what supports a marriage: fidelity, openness to children, forgiveness, prayer together, friends and community, and the sacraments (CCC 1643–1654). Two people who treat their promise as a project of daily choices find that freedom grows within it (CCC 1646–1651).',
    'Not everyone marries, but everyone makes promises: priests, religious, friends, workers. A promise is a way of making yourself trustworthy to people who will need you in a bad season. Even in a culture of options, a person who keeps their word is rare and valuable. And the Church’s teaching on the family as a “domestic church” (CCC 1655–1658) shows that vows are not private romance but a school of love for many others, children, neighbors, the parish.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What does a vow do that a feeling cannot?',
    'What is the difference between a contract and a covenant?',
    'How can a lifelong promise be a source of freedom?',
    'What other promises (not marriage) do you want to keep for life?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What makes a marriage, according to the Church?|A ceremony|The free consent of the spouses|A feeling of love|The wedding license|1|CCC 1626–1627.',
    'Why is marriage lifelong?|Tradition only|Because the love of spouses by its nature requires unity and indissolubility, a total gift|The law requires it|To avoid divorce|1|CCC 1643–1644.',
    'Is marriage only a human contract?|Yes|No, it is a covenant and, for the baptized, a sacrament that gives grace|It is a private arrangement|It is a legal fiction|1|CCC 1601, 1641.',
  ],
  life='Write a one-page letter to your future self (or to a future spouse or community) describing the kind of promise-keeper you want to be. Choose one promise you currently make (to a friend, a team, a job) and ask what keeping it fully would look like this month. Do it.',
  saint='Louis and Zélie Martin: a French watchmaker and a lacemaker, parents of nine children (including Thérèse of Lisieux), who in 2015 became the first married couple canonized together as a couple.',
  misc=[
    '“If the love is gone, the marriage is over.”',
    '“Vows take away freedom.”',
    '“Marriage is only for those who want children.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:marriage-divorce', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“Where you go I will go, and where you lodge I will lodge; your people shall be my people, and your God my God.” (Ruth 1:16)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain the difference between a contract and a covenant, and why a lifelong vow can be a gift of freedom.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask, “If you could undo, restart or swipe away anything with no consequence, what would you lose?” Take three answers, then read Ruth 1:16–17 aloud. Say, “Ruth could have gone home. She chose to stay. That is a vow.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Marriage in the Catholic view is a covenant, not a contract. A contract trades goods and can be ended when someone does not deliver. A covenant creates a family bond. The Catechism, 1601, calls marriage a partnership of the whole of life. The cause of marriage is consent: ‘I take you’. Feelings follow and grow. They are not the cause.”',
      'Say: “Why for life? Number 1644: the love of spouses by its nature requires unity and indissolubility. If love is a total gift, it has no expiry date. If I say ‘I am yours until I am not,’ I have not given you myself, I have loaned you something. Jesus says, what God has joined, let no one separate. The vow is what makes deep trust possible.”',
      'Say: “This is not naive. The Church knows vows are hard. That is why marriage is a sacrament; it gives grace for the covenant. And she names what carries it: fidelity, forgiveness, openness to children, prayer together, friends, the sacraments. A vow is a place where love gets room to grow, because you stop auditing the relationship and start building it.”',
      'Say: “And you do not have to marry to learn this. Everyone makes promises: to friends, a team, a job, a community. A person who keeps their word is rare and valuable. And remember the family is the domestic church, a school of love for others. Marriage is for the spouses, and also for everyone around them.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What would you want a person to promise you? What would you promise?',
      'What promises are you making now that you want to keep?',
      'Where have you seen a vow kept well?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out cards. Write the vow you would hope to hear and the one you would make.',
      'Underline words that cannot be kept by feeling alone.',
      'Write one promise you will keep this month and what it costs.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer to St. Joseph together. Say, “Love is a decision you keep making. Practise it in small things now.”'),
  tryit=['Write the vow you would want to hear and make.', 'Underline the words that need more than feeling.', 'Choose one promise to keep this month.'],
  saintStory='Louis Martin was born in 1823 and became a watchmaker in Alençon, France. Zélie Guérin was born in 1831; she became a lacemaker with a business of her own. Each had once thought of a religious vocation and been turned away. They met in 1858 and married three months later, on July 13.\nThey agreed to live as brother and sister at first, then were persuaded by a confessor to have children. They had nine; four died young, a loss that nearly broke Zélie. She ran her lace business, helped to support the family, and kept a stream of letters that still survive, full of humor, worry and faith. In 1877 she died of breast cancer at 45. Louis moved the family to Lisieux, raised his five daughters, and later suffered from a form of dementia. He died in 1894.\nAll five daughters entered religious life; the youngest was Thérèse of Lisieux. In 2015 Pope Francis canonized Louis and Zélie, the first married couple to be canonized together who were not martyrs. Their feast is July 12.',
),
])
